/*--------------------------------*- C++ -*----------------------------------*\
| SoPLASMA -- SHIPPED LIBRARY of solid dielectric materials                    |
|                                                                              |
| Governing directive G2: anything with literature values becomes a LIBRARY    |
| with cited numbers, not a number typed into a case.                          |
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       dictionary;
    object      dielectrics;
}
// * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * //

// HOW TO USE THIS
//
// In constant/<region>/electricalProperties write ONE line:
//
//     material    alumina96;
//
// and epsilonR and gammaSEE are resolved from here. PRECEDENCE, which follows
// G1 (derive, else default, else require): an explicit numeric `epsilonR` or
// `gammaSEE` in the case WINS -- it is an override, and it is reported as one --
// otherwise the material supplies it, otherwise the region KIND's default
// applies (1.0 for gas and farField; for a `dielectric` region epsilonR is
// required and there is no default).
//
// Naming a material that is not in this file is FATAL and lists what is here,
// rather than silently falling back to a default nobody chose.
//
// TO ADD A MATERIAL: add an entry with BOTH numbers and a `reference` string.
// An entry without a reference is a number somebody remembered, which is what
// this file exists to stop.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RELATIVE PERMITTIVITY
//
// Quoted at room temperature and at low frequency (~1 kHz - 1 MHz), which is
// the regime a plasma-fluid Poisson solve is in: the barrier responds
// quasi-statically to a discharge whose own timescale is ns, but epsilon_r for
// these materials is flat from DC into the MHz range, so a single number is
// appropriate. It is NOT appropriate at optical frequencies, and for the polar
// polymers (PMMA, polyimide) it drifts by 10-20% across the audio-to-MHz range.
//
// Values are handbook-level and mutually consistent across sources:
//   CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics, 95th ed. (2014), sec. 12
//   von Hippel, A. R., "Dielectric Materials and Applications" (MIT Press,
//     1954) -- still the standard tabulation for ceramics and glasses
//
// GAMMA (ion-induced secondary electron emission) IS THE LEAST TRANSFERABLE
// NUMBER IN THIS FILE.
//
// It depends on the ion species, its impact energy, and above all on the
// surface CONDITION -- adsorbed water, oxide, hydrocarbon contamination -- so a
// value measured on a sputter-cleaned sample in UHV does not describe the same
// material after an hour in room air. The spread across surface states is more
// than an order of magnitude, larger than any difference between the materials
// below.
//
// So every dielectric here carries 0.001, the contaminated-barrier figure, and
// that is deliberate: it is the same value and the same reasoning as the
// `defaultSEEC` default in the wall-flux conditions (changed to 0.001 on
// 2026-09-02), where the range is documented as ~1e-3 for contaminated oxides
// and dielectric barriers up to ~1e-1 for clean metals in vacuum. A library
// that disagreed with that default would make gamma depend on whether a case
// happened to name a material.
//
// MgO IS THE ONE REAL EXCEPTION and is marked as such.
//
// If gamma matters to your result -- in a barrier discharge it is what makes
// the discharge SELF-SUSTAINING rather than a single avalanche -- then it is a
// number to measure or to fit, not to take from a library. Override it in the
// case and say why.
//   Phelps, A. V. & Petrovic, Z. Lj. (1999) Plasma Sources Sci. Technol. 8, R21
//     -- the canonical treatment of effective gamma and its surface dependence
//   Raizer, Yu. P., "Gas Discharge Physics" (Springer, 1991), sec. 4
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

vacuum
{
    epsilonR    1.0;
    gammaSEE    0.0;
    reference   "Exact by definition. gammaSEE 0: there is no surface.";
}

air
{
    epsilonR    1.0;
    gammaSEE    0.0;
    // Use for a `farField` region -- a FICTITIOUS air region that extends
    // the electrostatic domain without extending the plasma one. Not a
    // surface, so gammaSEE 0.
    reference   "epsilon_r = 1.00059 at STP (CRC Handbook 95th ed.); 1.0 to the precision any plasma-fluid solve resolves.";
}

// --- polymers --------------------------------------------------------------

ptfe
{
    // NO bandGap/electronAffinity, deliberately: see the note above. The
    // insulator Auger criterion cannot be applied here, so an emission
    // model on this surface needs an explicit gamma.
    epsilonR    2.1;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 2.0-2.1, essentially flat from DC to 1 GHz -- non-polar,
    // so the lowest-permittivity practical solid barrier. CRC Handbook
    // 95th ed.; von Hippel (1954).
    reference   "PTFE (Teflon).";
}

pmma
{
    // NO bandGap/electronAffinity, deliberately: see the note above. The
    // insulator Auger criterion cannot be applied here, so an emission
    // model on this surface needs an explicit gamma.
    epsilonR    3.0;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 3.6-3.7 at 60 Hz falling to ~2.6 at 1 MHz as the polar
    // side-group relaxation drops out; 3.0 is representative near 1 MHz.
    // If the drive is in the kHz range use ~3.4. CRC Handbook 95th ed.;
    // von Hippel (1954).
    reference   "PMMA -- acrylic, Plexiglas, Perspex.";
}

polyimide
{
    // NO bandGap/electronAffinity, deliberately: see the note above. The
    // insulator Auger criterion cannot be applied here, so an emission
    // model on this surface needs an explicit gamma.
    epsilonR    3.4;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 3.4 at 1 kHz, 3.0 at 1 MHz. Common as a thin flexible
    // barrier, so often used with thinDielectricPotential rather than as a
    // meshed region. DuPont Kapton HN datasheet; CRC Handbook.
    reference   "Polyimide (Kapton HN).";
}

// --- glasses ---------------------------------------------------------------

fusedSilica
{
    bandGap          9.0;
    electronAffinity 0.9;
    // Eg = 8.9-9.3 eV across sources; chi = 0.8-1.1 eV. Auger threshold
    // Eg + 2chi = 10.8 eV. Refs: Weinberg, J. Appl. Phys. 53 (1982) 5052;
    // DiStefano & Eastman, Solid State Commun. 9 (1971) 2259.
    epsilonR    3.8;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 3.8, flat from DC to GHz. CRC Handbook 95th ed.; von
    // Hippel (1954).
    reference   "Fused silica / fused quartz (amorphous SiO2).";
}

borosilicateGlass
{
    // NO bandGap/electronAffinity, deliberately: see the note above. The
    // insulator Auger criterion cannot be applied here, so an emission
    // model on this surface needs an explicit gamma.
    epsilonR    4.6;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 4.6 at 1 MHz. The usual dielectric in laboratory DBD
    // reactors and plasma jets. von Hippel (1954); Corning 7740 datasheet.
    reference   "Borosilicate glass (Pyrex 7740).";
}

sodaLimeGlass
{
    // NO bandGap/electronAffinity, deliberately: see the note above. The
    // insulator Auger criterion cannot be applied here, so an emission
    // model on this surface needs an explicit gamma.
    epsilonR    7.0;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 6.9-7.5 at 1 MHz, higher and more loss-prone than
    // borosilicate because of the mobile alkali ions -- which also make it
    // drift with temperature. von Hippel (1954).
    reference   "Soda-lime (window) glass.";
}

mica
{
    // NO bandGap/electronAffinity, deliberately: see the note above. The
    // insulator Auger criterion cannot be applied here, so an emission
    // model on this surface needs an explicit gamma.
    epsilonR    5.4;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 5.4 (range 5.4-8.7 across micas and orientations -- it
    // is strongly anisotropic, and this solver treats every region as
    // ISOTROPIC, so a mica barrier is an approximation). CRC Handbook 95th
    // ed.; von Hippel (1954).
    reference   "Muscovite mica.";
}

// --- ceramics --------------------------------------------------------------

alumina96
{
    bandGap          8.8;
    electronAffinity 1.35;
    // Eg = 8.7-9.0 eV. chi IS BADLY SCATTERED, 1.0-2.5 eV across sources
    // and deposition routes; 1.35 is mid-range. Auger threshold ~11.5 eV,
    // so the spread in chi moves it by +/-1.5 eV -- treat the threshold as
    // indicative. Refs: French, J. Am. Ceram. Soc. 73 (1990) 477;
    // Robertson, J. Vac. Sci. Technol. B 18 (2000) 1785.
    epsilonR    9.0;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 9.0 at 1 MHz. The workhorse barrier for high-power and
    // high-temperature DBDs. von Hippel (1954); standard technical-ceramic
    // datasheets.
    reference   "96% Al2O3 ceramic.";
}

alumina99
{
    bandGap          8.8;
    electronAffinity 1.35;
    // As alumina96: the grade changes density and permittivity, not the
    // band structure.
    epsilonR    9.8;
    gammaSEE    0.001;
    // epsilon_r = 9.8 at 1 MHz -- denser and slightly higher than the 96%
    // grade. von Hippel (1954).
    reference   "99.5% Al2O3 ceramic.";
}

magnesia
{
    bandGap          7.8;
    electronAffinity 0.85;
    // Eg = 7.8 eV, chi = 0.85 eV -- an unusually LOW affinity, which is
    // what makes the Auger threshold Eg + 2chi = 9.5 eV low enough for Xe
    // and Ne, and hence why MgO is the protective layer in plasma display
    // panels. Refs: Whited & Walker, Phys. Rev. Lett. 22 (1969) 1428;
    // Robertson, J. Vac. Sci. Technol. B 18 (2000) 1785.
    epsilonR    9.7;
    gammaSEE    0.3;
    // epsilon_r = 9.7 (CRC Handbook 95th ed.). THE EXCEPTION ON GAMMA, AND
    // THE REASON MgO EXISTS IN THIS FILE. MgO is used as the protective
    // layer in plasma display panels precisely BECAUSE its ion-induced
    // secondary emission is exceptionally high -- of order 0.3 for Ne+ and
    // Xe+ at PDP energies, two to three orders of magnitude above a
    // contaminated barrier. That is what lowers the firing voltage enough
    // to make the device work. It is also strongly dependent on the ion
    // and on the surface state, and it DEGRADES in air. Treat 0.3 as an
    // order of magnitude for a fresh surface under noble-gas bombardment,
    // NOT as a number to carry into an air discharge. Boeuf, J. P. (2003)
    // J. Phys. D 36, R53.
    reference   "MgO.";
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BAND GAP AND ELECTRON AFFINITY -- the INSULATOR emission criterion
//
// For a METAL, ion-induced potential emission is governed by the work function:
// gamma_i ~ 0.016(0.8 E_ion - 2 phi), forbidden when 0.8 E_ion < 2 phi.
// phi IS NOT THE RIGHT QUANTITY FOR AN INSULATOR. There, Auger neutralisation
// must lift an electron from the VALENCE BAND across the gap AND over the
// electron affinity, so emission requires roughly
//
//     E_ion  >  Eg + 2 chi
//
// with Eg the band gap and chi the electron affinity. (Hagstrum's Auger
// analysis applied to insulators; see also Boeuf 2003 for the MgO case.)
//
// THIS IS WHY MgO IS EXCEPTIONAL, and it falls out of the numbers rather than
// being asserted: Eg = 7.8 eV with an unusually LOW chi = 0.85 eV gives a
// threshold of 9.5 eV, which Xe (12.13) and Ne (21.56) clear easily -- so a
// Xe/Ne display panel fires at a usable voltage. Alumina's threshold is
// 11.5 eV and silica's 10.8 eV, both far more restrictive.
//
// VALUES ARE GIVEN ONLY WHERE THEY ARE WELL ESTABLISHED -- the crystalline
// oxides. THEY ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT FOR THE POLYMERS AND GLASSES: a polymer
// has no crystalline band structure, its "gap" is an optical absorption edge
// that varies with formulation and ageing, and reliable electron affinities are
// not available. Rather than ship a number that cannot be defended, those
// entries carry none, and the emission model must be given a gamma explicitly
// for such a surface. That is the honest outcome, not a gap in the library.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KNOWN SIMPLIFICATION, TO BE FIXED: GAMMA IS UNIFORM HERE AND SHOULD NOT BE.
//
// Every dielectric below carries gammaSEE 0.001. That is a PLACEHOLDER, not a
// result, and it is being called out because a library in which 11 of 12
// entries share one value is not a library for that property -- it is a default
// wearing a library's clothes.
//
// The two reasons it is uniform today, stated so they can be argued with:
//   1. gamma is dominated by SURFACE CONDITION -- adsorbed water, oxide,
//      hydrocarbon contamination -- and the spread across surface states of ONE
//      material exceeds the spread between these materials.
//   2. 0.001 is the wall-flux `defaultSEEC` default, set on 2026-09-02 with a
//      dated justification. A library that disagreed with it would make gamma
//      depend on whether a case happened to name a material.
//
// WHY THAT IS NOT GOOD ENOUGH. There IS genuine material dependence, and it is
// not small: for potential emission gamma tracks the WORK FUNCTION for metals
// and the BAND GAP plus ELECTRON AFFINITY for insulators, which is precisely
// why MgO reaches 0.3 while a fluoropolymer does not. Flattening that to one
// number discards physics the solver could represent.
//
// WHAT THE FIX LOOKS LIKE (deferred by agreement 2026-09-04, to be done AFTER
// the boundary-role library and layer 1, because it needs the patch-material
// route those provide):
//   * gamma per material AND per incident ion, since gamma_i depends on the ion
//     as much as on the surface -- so the entry becomes a small table, not a
//     scalar;
//   * an explicit SURFACE-STATE qualifier (as-received / air-exposed / baked /
//     sputter-cleaned) rather than pretending one number covers them;
//   * ENERGY dependence, at least the linear potential-emission form for
//     metals, gamma ~ 0.016*(0.8*E_i - 2*phi) (Raizer 1991, sec. 4);
//   * the same treatment for ELECTRODES, which today cannot name a material at
//     all.
// Until then, a case for which gamma matters must state it explicitly, and
// should say why in a comment.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// NOT IN THIS FILE, DELIBERATELY: conductors.
//
// An electrode's gamma is a property of the electrode, and there is currently
// no way for a case to say what a metal PATCH is made of -- that is the layer-1
// boundary work (G2), not yet built. Shipping copper/steel/aluminium entries
// here would be a library nothing can reach, so they are omitted until the
// patch-material route exists. Until then an electrode's gamma is set with
// `defaultSEEC` on its wall-flux condition, which defaults to 0.001.

// ************************************************************************* //
